<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Knowth</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 3200 BCE</span></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Knowth is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neolithic">Neolithic</a> passage tomb complex in the Boyne valley, County Meath, Ireland, built around 3200 BCE and part of the Brú na Bóinne <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/world-heritage-site">World Heritage Site</a>. Its great mound covers two passage tombs and is ringed by smaller satellite tombs, and it holds the largest collection of megalithic art in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-europe">Western Europe</a>. In the early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> the kings of northern Brega made it a royal residence.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/knowth.jpg" alt="Knowth" width="320" height="400" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Markiemcg1 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:County_Meath_-_Knowth_-_20210930145613.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The great mound, 9 kilometres west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/drogheda">Drogheda</a>, is about 12 metres high and 67 metres across, covers roughly a hectare and is surrounded by 17 smaller tombs. It is ringed by 127 kerbstones. Its two passages run east and west to separate chambers: the eastern one to a cruciform chamber with three recesses and basin stones that held the cremated dead, the western one to an undifferentiated chamber. Their orientation suggests an alignment with the equinoxes, though later disturbance makes it hard to establish.</p><p>Over 200 decorated stones were found. Besides spirals, lozenges and serpentine forms they include crescents and what is held to be the oldest known depiction of the moon, and much of the carving is on the hidden backs of stones, either deliberately concealed or reused.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>After the mound fell into disuse, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timber">timber</a> circle near the eastern entrance served as a ritual site in the late <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neolithic">Neolithic</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bronze-age">Bronze Age</a>, and some 35 cist graves were later dug there, but the site then lay largely unused for about two thousand years. In the late <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-age">Iron Age</a> and early Christian period it became a hill fort with ditches and souterrains, its passages were entered and four names were cut in ogham, and it grew into the capital of the Kingdom of Northern Brega. The medieval Triads of Ireland list its caves among the three darkest places in Ireland.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/normans">Normans</a> used it as a motte in the 12th century; it then passed to the Cistercian monks of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mellifont-abbey">Mellifont Abbey</a> as a grange, and after the dissolution of the monasteries it was farmland until the state took over most of the site in 1939.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>George Eogan of University College Dublin led excavations from 1962, finding the western and eastern passages in 1967 and 1968, and parts of the monument were then reconstructed. Unlike at Newgrange, the white quartz found at the entrances was left on the ground, a decision some archaeologists dispute. The site is visited by guided tour from the Brú na Bóinne Visitor Centre at Donore.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Knowth” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In Ireland the arrival of the Beaker culture was accompanied by the destruction of smaller tombs at <b>Knowth</b> and collapses of the great cairn at Newgrange, marking the end of the Neolithic tradition of megalithic passage tombs.</blockquote><a href="/culture/bell-beaker-culture">Bell Beaker culture · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>Their rulers made the ancient passage tomb at <b>Knowth</b> into a royal residence.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-brega">Kingdom of Brega</a></li></ul></section>
